speechtext:
The proposed amendment to this section will also be found in the Votes and Proceedings. It is to cover particularly the suggestions made for the improvement of the section by .the leader of the opposition (Mr. Bennett), the hon. member for St. Law-rence-St. George (Mr. Caban), and one suggestion made, I think, by the hon. member for Rosthern (Mr. Tucker). It is proposed to amend the section as follows:
That clause 8 of the bill be struck out and the following substituted therefor:
"8. (1) Subject to the provisions of this act, the governor in council may by regulation:

Home Improvement Loans
(a)	prescribe the forms of applications for home improvement loans, notes, agreements, certificates and any other documents necessary or desirable for the effective operation of this act;
(b)	provide that, in the event of actual or impending default by a borrower, an approved lending institution may, notwithstanding anything contained in section 6 of this act, with the approval of the borrower alter or revise by way of extension of time or otherwise, any of the terms of his agreement, and that such alteration or revision shall not operate to discharge any guarantee given under this act, and in the event of default by a borrower the legal and other measures to be taken and the procedure to be followed for the collection of the amount of the home improvement loan outstanding and the rate of interest to be charged on payments which are overdue;
(c)	provide for the determination of the amount of loss sustained by an approved lending institution and the procedure to be followed in making claims in respect of any guarantee given under this act;
(d)	prescribe tables showing maximum charges for loans of varying terms and varying methods of repayment;
(e)	require reports to be made periodically to the minister by approved lending institutions;
(f)	define for the purposes of this act the following expressions:
(i)	"owner" with power to include as owners life tenants, persons holding property under agreements for sale or under long term leases and any other persons having rights approximating ownership;
(ii)	"rural and urban homes" with power to include in such expression all such property as may reasonably be considered as part of a rural or urban home;
(iii)	"repairs, alterations and additions';
(g)	make provision for any other matters concerning which regulations are deemed necessary or desirable to carry out the purposes and intention of this act.
(2) Regulations made hereunder shall, when published in the Canada Gazette, have the same force and effect as if they had been included in this act.
Mr. MACKENZIE (Vancouver); I move accordingly,. Mr. Chairman,

The problem with which the original legislation dealt is at this time one of extreme gravity to something like three hundred municipalities in the three western provinces. At the time the legislation was passed in 1935, the number of municipalities affected was not so large as it is now, but in the great provinces of Saskatchewan and Alberta practically 250 municipalities are affected by this legislation, a number of which, added to the municipalities in Manitoba, makes a total of nearly three hundred municipalities, a vast area in the three western provinces, that may be described as being almost incapable of producing crop at the present time. I believe that over a period of years these municipalities have not produced a crop. I have not the figures before me, so I am not in a position to state what period of time has elapsed during which they have produced no substantial crop, but certainly in Saskatchewan and in a portion of Alberta that period is in excess of five years. As to how long it may be in Manitoba I have no recollection, and therefore I cannot speak with any authority.
I rise to point out the seriousness of this situation. It is so serious as to have ceased to be regarded in any sense as a local matter. In the truest andl largest sense of the term it is national. I have heard various suggestions made, some by men with a wide scientific knowledge, as to whether or not we can look for any rehabilitation of some of these areas. I have newer been regarded as a pessimist with respect to these matters, but I confess to this committee that during the last twelve months I have become extremely apprehensive about the situation. That fact does not make it any more or less important; I merely record my opinion. When I last saw that area, in the great heat of the closing part of the month of July and the early part of August, it was calculated to depress the spirits of almost any Canadian who looked at it. The minister of that day closely investigated the matter, and came to his colleagues with certain recommendations and suggestions. As a result of the suggestions and recommendations, the statute chapter 23 of the statutes for 1935, was enacted. The minister now suggests that it is desirable to amend the statute by creating advisory councils in local areas.

Farm Rehabilitation Act

I am bound to say that the minister of that diay, supported as he was by those with whom he had conferred, was of opinion that it was wrong to endeavour to give a purely local bent to the efforts which were being made, and that in the largest and best sense of the term we should secure, as an advisory committee, representative men in all of the provinces who would deal with the problem as one common to all, and not as one which affected merely local interests in the municipalities concerned. If the government are of opinion that the best purpose will be served by setting up small advisory committees in the municipalities, it is not for me, without having the knowledge that they possess, to offer objection. But I do say this: I was struck at the time, and I still am, with the fact that it may be possible to create purely local interest as distinct from what I conceive to be in the truest and best sense of the term an interest which is widespread, which concerns the whole area, which is concerned, not with the fortunes of individuals but with the fortunes of the whole three hundred municipalities, and the large numbers of people residing in them.
Having in mind the purpose the then minister had in view when making the recommendations, recommendations which were not made until he had conferred with many people, we find the advisory council was to comprise certain members whom I shall indicate shortly. The committee was known as an advisory committee, and it owed its origin to the governor in council. One of the members was to be appointed chairman by the governor in council, and the committee was to consist of the following:
(a)	One representative of the Manitoba grain growing farmers from the drought and soil drifting areas;
(b)	One representative of the Saskatchewan grain growing farmers from the drought and soil drifting areas;
(c)	One representative of the Alberta grain growing farmers from the drought and soil drifting areas;
(d)	One representative of Saskatchewan livestock farmers from the drought areas;
(e)	One representative of Alberta range farmers from the drought areas;
(f)	One representative of mortgage companies of Canada.
That is an organization which, while having no statutory being, is a voluntary organization of mortgage companies of the country. In addition there were to be the following:
(g)	One representative of the Canadian Bankers' Association;
(h)	One representative each from the Canadian Pacific Railway Company and the Canadian National Railways;
(i)	Two representatives from the Dominion Department of Agriculture; and

(j)	One representative of the government in each of the provinces of Manitoba, Saskatchewan and Alberta.
The committee, hon. members will observe, is in no sense local. It was believed-and I think the belief has been wholly warranted by the results-that the committee would take a broad view of the situation, and that Manitoba would be deeply interested in what affected settlers in the drought and soil drifting areas of Manitoba or Alberta, and vice versa.
To set up a local committee in a municipality will, I trust, be a success. I think, however, it is only fair to point out-
